/**
 * BREAST CANCER: THE UK AND NHS LAYER, KEYED TO THE FAMILY. Facts checked 2026-09-25 against the pages cited on
 * each row.
 *
 * This is the shared NHS story: the part that is the same whichever receptor result comes back. It is keyed to
 * `breast-cancer` with the receptor subtypes and the histologies as aliases, so a woman who has just been told
 * lands on one page and is routed from it. What is specific to a receptor subtype stays on that subtype's page:
 * the triple-negative pathway is at /cancers/tnbc/uk/ and this page does not restate it.
 *
 * What is here and nowhere else in the corpus: the NHS Breast Screening Programme in full, which is the oldest
 * and largest cancer screening programme in the country and had no page at all, with the 2024/25 annual figures
 * read from NHS England's own release of them, the current leaflet's recall arithmetic, the programme's own
 * performance standards and the Marmot review's benefit and overdiagnosis estimate read from the UK National
 * Screening Committee's own page; the very high risk surveillance programme with its gene-by-gene and age-by-age
 * protocol; the family history service from the NICE CG164 referral criteria through the R208 eligibility
 * criteria in the National Genomic Test Directory to the risk-reducing options with their recommendation numbers;
 * the diagnostic pathway with the National Audit of Primary Breast Cancer's finding on the one-stop clinic; the
 * waiting-time figures by route for both breast pathways, which no other page in the corpus carries because
 * breast is the only cancer with two; the NICE positions that apply whatever the receptor status, including three
 * refusals and two appraisals abandoned because the company did not submit; and where the four nations differ.
 *
 * No figure is remembered: each carries the page it was read from. Where a page could not be read, the gap is
 * named with the response the server gave.
 */
